 "claims": [
  {
   "id": "C1",
   "statement": "SCOPED BY C12. On the four RELEASED candidate responses, less than half of a rubric's ability to predict held-out human rankings is attributable to prompt-specific criterion content; the remainder is generic response quality obtainable with an unrelated rubric. C12 shows this prompt-specific component does not extend to responses the criteria were not authored against, so C1 must not be read as a property of the rubric in general.",
   "source": "rounds/r04_rebuild_satisfaction/results/a04_full.json",
   "field": "rubric_contribution_share",
   "value": 0.42527201121420455,
   "test": "< 0.5",
   "status": "HOLDS"
  },
  {
   "id": "C2",
   "statement": "The rebuilt criterion-satisfaction layer predicts held-out human pairwise rankings above chance and above a length-only baseline.",
   "source": "rounds/r04_rebuild_satisfaction/results/a04_full.json",
   "field": "pairwise_accuracy",
   "value": 0.6860023341858906,
   "test": "> 0.55",
   "status": "HOLDS"
  },
  {
   "id": "C3",
   "statement": "Among defensible aggregation principles at k=4, out-of-sample predictive accuracy spans less than 3 percentage points, and a lower-quartile consensus rule is indistinguishable from random selection of four shared criteria.",
   "source": "rounds/r06_rule_tournament/results/a06_rule_tournament.json",
   "field": "rules.consensus.accuracy",
   "value": 0.6387379246267612,
   "test": "~ 0.64",
   "status": "HOLDS"
  },
  {
   "id": "C4",
   "statement": "Beyond an additive per-rater (actor) effect, pairwise rater agreement carries a PAIR-specific component that persists across disjoint prompt sets above a dyad-permutation null. The actor component is the larger of the two; the pair-specific residual is roughly a quarter of the raw persistence r01 reported, and it is the only part that bears on whether a single core rubric is the wrong object.",
   "source": "rounds/r23_actor_vs_dyad/results/r23_actor_vs_dyad.json",
   "field": "style_removed.resid_z",
   "value": 4.667747516877456,
   "test": "> 2.0",
   "status": "HOLDS"
  },
  {
   "id": "C5",
   "statement": "Anthropomorphic style independently predicts human preference after controlling for the rubric score and response length, while fewer than 1 percent of crowd-written criteria address it.",
   "source": "rounds/r07_anthropomorphism/results/a07_anthropomorphism.json",
   "field": "rubric_absorption_test.anthro_t",
   "value": 4.022353963516904,
   "test": "> 2.0",
   "status": "HOLDS"
  },
  {
   "id": "C14",
   "statement": "Fewer than one percent of crowd-written CoVal-full criteria address anthropomorphic self-presentation, under a word-boundary lexicon whose Tier-1 term list is known to over-count: at least 13 of its 24 matches are off-construct or polarity-reversed, so this rate is an upper bound.",
   "source": "rounds/r07_anthropomorphism/results/a07_anthropomorphism.json",
   "field": "criteria_tier1_share",
   "value": 0.0015739769150052466,
   "test": "< 0.01",
   "status": "HOLDS"
  },
  {
   "id": "C6",
   "statement": "PRE-REGISTERED AND REFUTED: optimizing selection against the rubric was predicted to raise lexical overlap with the criterion text (the gaming direction implied by C1). Test compares overlap at max strength against overlap at n=1; the prediction requires a rise of at least 0.02.",
   "source": "rounds/r09_overoptimization/results/a09_overoptimization.json",
   "field": "overlap_rise",
   "value": -0.022924511162629946,
   "test": "> 0.02",
   "status": "FAILS"
  },
  {
   "id": "C7",
   "statement": "RETRACTED BY C11. Within best-of-16 pressure the gold preference change is distinguishable from zero. This held only with a gold head sharing the judge's backbone; the independent-backbone control (C11) does not reproduce it, so the effect is NOT established in either direction.",
   "source": "rounds/r11_backbone_control/results/a11_backbone_control.json",
   "field": "gold_08b_independent.delta",
   "value": 0.04986555007180198,
   "test": "> 0.3",
   "status": "FAILS"
  },
  {
   "id": "C11",
   "statement": "The A09 result is reproduced by a gold preference head built on a DIFFERENT backbone from the judge, ruling out shared-backbone leakage.",
   "source": "rounds/r11_backbone_control/results/a11_backbone_control.json",
   "field": "independent_reproduces",
   "value": false,
   "test": "== True",
   "status": "FAILS"
  },
  {
   "id": "C9",
   "statement": "The attribution decomposition is not an artifact of one judge or one prompt template: across three judge/template configurations the prompt-specific contribution stays positive with small spread.",
   "source": "rounds/r10_attribution_robustness/results/a10_attribution.json",
